<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sunan Gunung Jati</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Syarif Hidayatullah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sunan Gunung Jati was one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wali-songo">Wali Songo</a>, the Nine Saints credited with spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. He is regarded as the founder of the Cirebon Sultanate and, with his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maulana-hasanuddin">Maulana Hasanuddin</a>, led the forces of Demak and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banten">Banten</a> from the Sunda Kingdom around 1527. His tomb near Cirebon remains a major pilgrimage site.</p></div>
